WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
The Rule distinguishes a security event from a notification event.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:14.680
A security event broadly involves unauthorized access to, disruption of, or misuse of an information system, information stored in it, or customer information held physically.

3
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:24.540
A written incident-response plan prepares the institution to respond to a security event materially affecting the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of customer information.

4
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:40.500
The plan must define goals, internal response processes, clear roles and decision authority, internal and external communications, remediation of identified weaknesses, documentation and reporting, and evaluation and revision after an event.

5
00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:51.080
Preparation should also establish escalation routes, evidence preservation, legal coordination, provider participation, and access to reliable inventories.

6
00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:58.640
A notification event is narrower: acquisition of unencrypted customer information without the authorization of the person to whom it pertains.

7
00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:05.060
Encrypted information is considered unencrypted when an encryption key was accessed by an unauthorized person.

8
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:16.200
Unauthorized access to unencrypted customer information is presumed to include unauthorized acquisition unless reliable evidence shows that acquisition did not occur or could not reasonably have occurred.

9
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:27.200
When a notification event involves information of at least five hundred consumers, the institution must notify the Federal Trade Commission as soon as possible and no later than thirty days after discovery.

10
00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.660
Discovery is the first day the event is known to the institution.

11
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:38.200
Knowledge is attributed when an employee, officer, or other agent—other than the person committing the breach—knows of it.

12
00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.100
Internal delay does not postpone discovery.

13
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:55.620
The electronic notice includes the institution's name and contact information, information types involved, dates when determinable, the number affected or potentially affected, a general description, and specified law-enforcement information.

14
00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.880
Reports may become public.

15
00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.100
Delay requires the process described by the Rule.

16
00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.020
The provision concerns notice to the Commission.

17
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:15.800
It does not replace separate analysis of notice to individuals, state agencies, other regulators, partners, or affected financial institutions under other law or contract.

18
00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:21.500
Not every security event is reportable, but every event needs disciplined assessment and evidence.
